  pages={7081-7088},
  abstract={Oral cancer poses a major global health concern, and the early recognition of potentially malignant lesions remains a persistent challenge in clinical practice. This study introduces an integrated artificial intelligence framework that advances beyond prior works by unifying lesion detection, visual explanation, and language-based reporting into a single multimodal pipeline designed to enhance diagnostic accuracy, transparency, and communication in oral oncology. A total of 1,271 intraoral images collected from multiple public datasets were obtained from publicly available repositories. All source datasets provided binary labels (cancer vs. healthy), and the entire dataset was manually annotated by a trained clinician to ensure consistent and reliable ground-truth labels. The detection model, based on the YOLOv11 architecture, was trained to localize abnormal or malignant mucosal regions, and interpretability was achieved through Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM), whose heatmaps were reviewed by oral pathology experts to confirm clinical relevance. To bridge the gap between automated analysis and clinical communication, the lightweight DistilGPT-2 language model was integrated to convert visual outputs into coherent narrative summaries, enabling explainable reporting and facilitating human understanding of AI-derived findings. The YOLOv11 model achieved a precision of 0.825, a recall of 0.63, a mean average precision of 0.72, and an $\text{mAP} {@} 0.5-0.95$ of 0.321, indicating accurate lesion localization but also highlighting limitations in recall. Grad-CAM visualizations showed substantial overlap with clinically relevant mucosal areas, supporting interpretability and diagnostic consistency, while DistilGPT-2 generated concise and clinically coherent reports. Overall, the combined use of YOLOv11 for detection, Grad-CAM for visual explanation, and DistilGPT-2 for textual interpretation provides a reproducible multimodal framework that enhances transparency and supports clinicians in the detection and assessment of oral cancer lesions.},
