  abstract={Precise temporomandibular joint (TMJ) condyle segmentation is vital for TMJ disorder morphological analysis and clinical management. Complex condylar anatomy and medical imaging noise/low - contrast issues challenge accurate semantic segmentation; conventional CNN/Transformer models have trouble with long - range dependencies and anatomical shape consistency during efficient computation. We introduce 3D Condyle-Mamba, a hybrid architecture combining U - Net, Mamba state space models, and shape priors, with a CNN - Mamba encoder - decoder for local/global context, Mamba Shape Module (MSM) ensuring anatomical consistency via attention masks and Edge-guided Spatial and Channel Attention (ESCA) enhancing boundary perception with Laplacian priors. Clinical CBCT dataset tests show 3D Condyle-Mamba's top tier performance (IOU 93.26, Dice 96.50) outperforming others in Precision/Recall/F1; ablation experiments find MSM reduces segmentation errors for better surface curvature. Qualitative evaluations highlight its boundary robustness and low - contrast resistance. This study creates a new medical image analysis framework for physiological priors with potential in TMJ disorder diagnosis/surgery.},
  keywords={Image segmentation;Solid modeling;Three-dimensional displays;Laplace equations;Shape;Surface morphology;Computer architecture;Robustness;Medical diagnostic imaging;Context modeling;Medical image segmentation;shape prior;Feature fusion;Edge Attention},
